Tiles for the things Angband does not have
==========================================

    audit-tiles.py     what each set does not draw; fails if there is a gap
    borrow-tiles.py    give a thing with no tile one that already exists
    make-terrain.py    draw the wilderness for the sets that have none
    make-services.py   draw the six service buildings
    build-neon.py      build the Neon set, which is generated whole

Run the audit first.  It is the only one that answers the question that
matters -- is anything in the game still without a tile -- and it exits
non-zero if the answer is yes.  The other scripts each check what they wrote,
which is a different and smaller claim.

Where the gaps come from
------------------------

ZangbandTK adds 389 monsters, seventeen terrain features, six places to walk
into that are not shops, and a run of objects; Angband's four tilesets have art
for none of it, because Angband has none of it.  In graphics mode every one of
those rendered as an ASCII letter in the middle of a drawn dungeon.

Three mechanisms close that, in increasing cost:

**Borrow** -- `borrow-tiles.py`.  Most of it.  A thing with no tile takes the
picture of a thing that has one: monsters match on `base:`, preferring the same
`color:` and then the nearest `depth:`; objects match on `type:`, then colour,
then nearest `level:`; traps match on the code trap.txt groups them under.  No
art is drawn.  It writes `graf-ztk.prf` into each set and adds one `%:` line so
the set loads it.

**Draw a rule** -- `make-terrain.py` for ground, `make-services.py` for the six
services.  Reached when nothing similar exists to borrow from.  Both generate
into a row appended to the set's own sheet, sampling that set's own palette, so
the new art sits in the set rather than beside it -- and both reclaim their row
on a re-run, so running them twice is the same as running them once.  Being
generated has a licensing dividend as well: the art is this project's own.

**Draw a picture** -- not done for these sets, and not planned.  Four sheets at
three tile sizes is four times the work and four chances to be inconsistent.

Uniques are left alone
----------------------

Deliberately, and it is the most important thing here.  A borrowed tile is a
fair stand-in for a common monster and a lie about a named one -- and the lie
is bad in a specific way.  Matched on base and depth, Oberon of Amber, Dworkin
Barimen and Mandor all come out wearing the Mouth of Sauron's face.  For a game
whose whole purpose is to move away from that, three Amber princes in Tolkien's
clothing is worse than no picture at all.  An ASCII letter says "not drawn
yet".  A wrong portrait says something false.

Around 120 uniques per set therefore render as text, and the audit does not
count that as a gap.

Nothing else here makes a claim that strong, which is why BASE_ALIAS exists.
Angband has no fish, no wall monsters and no animated weapons, so those had
nothing to borrow and used to be letters; a fish drawn as a snake asserts only
that it is long and legless, which it is.

Four ways this goes wrong in silence
------------------------------------

Every one of these produced a file that parsed cleanly and did nothing:

* **Case.**  The engine compares names with `my_stricmp`, and the shipped files
  do not agree with the data: the prf files say "Filthy street urchin" where
  monster.txt says "filthy street urchin".  A case-sensitive check reports 629
  missing monsters in a set that draws all of Angband.
* **Object names.**  `lookup_sval()` compares against
  `obj_desc_name_format()` of each kind's name, so `& Small wooden chest~` has
  to be written "Small wooden chest".  The raw form resolves to nothing, and an
  sval that does not exist is not an error.
* **Substring monster names.**  `lookup_monster()` falls back to a substring
  match, so a misspelt name binds to whatever contains it.
* **Coordinates.**  The renderer takes the cell index modulo the sheet size
  rather than complaining, so a coordinate that is not really in the sheet
  draws some other picture.

`--check` on each script is what proves these stayed avoided.  Names come from
the data files and borrowed coordinates are copied from lines the set already
uses, so all four are avoided by construction -- but by construction is a claim
that needs testing, not asserting.

Replacing a placeholder with real art
-------------------------------------

Delete the line from the generated file and add a proper mapping to the set's
own prf.  Re-running will not put it back: a generated line only ever covers a
thing that had no tile from any other file.

One ordering rule
-----------------

`make-terrain.py` and `make-services.py` both append a row to the same sheet,
and terrain's row is above services'.  If terrain is re-run it must be run
first, because it truncates the sheet back to its own row.
`make-services.py --check` says so if it happens.
